Use Bayes’ theorem / confusion-matrix logic:

\[
\text{PPV}=\frac{\text{Sensitivity}\times \text{Prevalence}}
{\text{Sensitivity}\times \text{Prevalence}+(1-\text{Specificity})\times(1-\text{Prevalence})}
\]

Here:

- Sensitivity = 0.95
- Specificity = 0.90, so false positive rate = 0.10

### (a) Prevalence = 1% = 0.01

\[
\text{PPV}=\frac{0.95(0.01)}{0.95(0.01)+0.10(0.99)}
=\frac{0.0095}{0.0095+0.099}
=\frac{0.0095}{0.1085}
\approx 0.08756
\]

**PPV ≈ 8.76%**

### (b) Prevalence = 0.1% = 0.001

\[
\text{PPV}=\frac{0.95(0.001)}{0.95(0.001)+0.10(0.999)}
=\frac{0.00095}{0.00095+0.0999}
=\frac{0.00095}{0.10085}
\approx 0.00942
\]

**PPV ≈ 0.94%**

### Why PPV changes

PPV drops sharply as prevalence falls because there are far fewer actual fraud cases relative to legitimate transactions. Even with the same 10% false positive rate, the much larger number of legitimate transactions produces many false alarms, so a flagged transaction is much less likely to be truly fraudulent.

